3. DOMAIN SEMANTICS THE UI MUST HONOUR

These rules exist because the benchmark deliberately contains look-alike cases. Getting them wrong makes the product misleading.

3.1 Decisions are typed — never collapse them

DecisionType: APPROVAL, CONDITIONAL_APPROVAL, LIMITED_SCOPE_ACCEPTANCE, REJECTION, RECOMMENDATION, SELECTION, CONFIRMATION.

A RECOMMENDATION is not an approval (Head of Department recommends → Dean approves). Show a distinct label and the person's capacity ("Recommended by", "Approved by", "Confirmed by").
A CONDITIONAL_APPROVAL shows its conditions, each with a state: FULFILLED (with the evidence email), PENDING, or NOT_EVIDENCED.
A LIMITED_SCOPE_ACCEPTANCE shows "Authorizes" and "Does not authorize" lists side by side. "Accepted for budget planning only" must never read as purchase authorisation.
An approval ceiling ("up to INR 28,000") is a ceiling, not an actual spend.
A relayed statement (e.g., Purchase reporting that Accounts confirmed a budget) shows attribution_note.
3.2 Approval ≠ completion

Show decision state and execution state separately. An approved purchase with "delivery and installation pending" is Awaiting action, not Completed.

3.3 Time
Store/transport UTC. Display in the organisation timezone (default Asia/Kolkata, labelled "IST") with a one-click "show UTC" toggle and the exact UTC value in a tooltip.
Event date ≠ message date: when text says "transferred on 4 September" in a message sent on 5 September, show both ("Event 4 Sep · reported 5 Sep").
In-text times keep their label (e.g., "12:30 IST"); never relabel them as UTC.
A date-only deadline means end of day in the organisation timezone.
deadline_state: NONE, UPCOMING, OVERDUE, MET, MISSED, UNDETERMINED. UNDETERMINED (e.g., delivered but no delivery date stated) is shown as such — never as late or on time.
Relative deadlines ("within eight weeks of order confirmation") are shown as text; never convert them to an invented calendar date.
All "latest / overdue / upcoming" logic uses an as_of time. In demo mode as_of = 2026-10-30T11:00:00Z (end of the corpus); in live mode use the server's as_of if supplied, else the current time. Always show the as_of where status is shown.
3.4 Money
Format with Indian digit grouping (en-IN): INR 17,90,000. Always keep the original text ("INR 17.9 lakh") available beside the normalised value (1,790,000), via a global "Amounts: original / normalised / both" preference.
Label each amount's role (Quoted, Estimate, Ceiling, Approved, Paid, Total, Cap, Planned, Eligible) and basis (per unit / total).
Derived calculations (e.g., 28,000 + 6,500 = 34,500) are shown as a calculation block: operands, each linked to its source citation, then the result, labelled "Derived calculation". Never show an unexplained sum.
3.5 People and roles

Show role (e.g., "Dean (Administration)") with the address on hover. If role_source is INFERRED_FROM_ADDRESS, show a small "role inferred from address" marker with an explanatory tooltip. Never invent a person's name.

3.6 Provenance

Every claim, decision and action carries provenance: EXPLICIT (stated in an email), DERIVED_CALC (computed from cited operands), INFERRED (model judgement). Use consistent visual treatment everywhere: EXPLICIT = solid, DERIVED = hatched/“calc” marker, INFERRED = dashed outline + "Inferred" label. An inferred item must never be the sole basis for an approval, amount or owner.

3.7 Unknown is a valid value (display rules)
Situation	Show
No owner stated	"Owner not stated"
No deadline stated	"No deadline stated"
Thread ended complete, no pending item	"No pending action stated in the emails" (distinct from "unknown")
Completion not evidenced	"No completion evidence in the available emails"
Backend did not report a status/field	"Not reported"
Nothing found	"I could not find sufficient supporting information in the available email records."
3.8 Answer statuses

ANSWERED, PARTIAL, INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE, CONFLICTING_EVIDENCE, CLARIFICATION_NEEDED, DEGRADED (retrieval degraded, e.g., "semantic search unavailable; keyword results shown"), FAILED (processing error). Each has a distinct icon + text + colour banner (never colour alone). Do not show any numeric "confidence score". If the backend supplies confidence.level (HIGH/MEDIUM/LOW) with reasons, show it labelled "Evidence confidence (defined rubric)" with the reasons; otherwise show nothing.

3.9 Thread status classes

OPEN, AWAITING_ACTION, PARTIALLY_COMPLETE, MONITORING, COMPLETED, UNKNOWN (each with icon + text). MONITORING means completion depends on an observation period.

3.10 Access model (frontend responsibilities)

Visibility is enforced only by the backend. The UI must: never reveal titles, counts or hints for inaccessible content; treat 404 as "not available" (neutral, same wording as "not found"); never claim permissions it cannot enforce. In demo mode, personas apply a client-side illustration of "thread participant" visibility, clearly labelled "Demo persona — visibility illustrated in the browser, not enforced".

