5. MOTION AND 3D
5.1 General

Framer Motion for route transitions (≤ 200 ms fade/translate-4px), drawers, list reveals, micro-interactions; SVG/CSS for light indicators; React Three Fiber + Three.js only in the three meaningful scenes below, lazy-loaded (React.lazy + Suspense), mounted only when visible (IntersectionObserver), paused when off-screen or the tab is hidden. Respect prefers-reduced-motion (no parallax, no looping; show a settled static frame). A single useCapability() hook decides 3D vs fallback: fall back to the SVG/CSS alternative when WebGL is unavailable, prefers-reduced-motion is set, viewport width < 768 px, navigator.hardwareConcurrency ≤ 4, navigator.deviceMemory ≤ 4 (when exposed), or the user disables "3D visuals" in Settings. Cap dpr at 1.75. Use instancing; ≤ ~60 animated objects per scene. Provide a text alternative (aria-label/visually-hidden description); canvases are aria-hidden and non-focusable. If a 3D dependency cannot be resolved in this environment, ship the SVG/CSS fallback and continue — never leave the app broken.

5.2 Scene A — Landing hero ("the thread connects")

Colours: forest ground, ivory message plates, one gold thread, one gold central node. Content: 52 small rounded message plates grouped into 10 clusters of sizes 7, 5, 6, 6, 4, 6, 6, 4, 4, 4 (the real benchmark thread sizes), and one continuous thin gold thread (a Catmull-Rom curve through the cluster centres, rendered as a thin tube) with a gold central node (wireframe ring) at the centre. Timeline (seconds, then loop gently): 0–2 plates appear scattered with subtle drift; 2–5 the thread draws itself through the cluster centres; 5–8 plates ease into their clusters while thin connection lines fade in between consecutive messages in each cluster; 8+ the structure settles, with a very slow orbit (±8°) and a faint 'breathing'; every ~24 s the plates loosen slightly and re-settle (smooth, never a hard reset). Desktop pointer gives ≤ 3° parallax (disabled on touch/reduced motion). Fallback: an SVG composition of the same structure with CSS stroke-dashoffset thread-draw animation.

5.3 Scene B — Workflow ("from messages to intelligence")

A sticky, scroll-driven scene with four stages revealed by scroll progress and by four visible step buttons (keyboard and touch accessible): 1 Email messages (scattered plates) → 2 Connected conversations (plates gather into thread clusters, connecting lines) → 3 Extracted decisions (selected nodes within clusters turn gold) → 4 Institutional intelligence (clusters link to the gold central node). Synchronise a caption panel for each stage. The landing page text may mention "illustrated with the supplied synthetic benchmark (52 emails, 10 conversations)" but must not show any other statistic. Reduced-motion/low-power fallback: a static four-panel SVG diagram.

5.4 Scene C — Intelligence constellation (Overview page; real data, informative)

SVG-first (2.5D with layered parallax), optional lazy 3D toggle. Nodes = emails; clusters = threads (labelled with thread key and title); edges = in_reply_to relationships; node colour by thread status; gold ring = the email carries a decision (derived from the decisions' evidence); hover/focus tooltip (email ID, sender role, local time); click opens the Evidence drawer; legend always visible; keyboard operable; accessible table alternative ("View as list"). It must be computed from the data actually loaded and must never be decorative.

5.5 Thread relationship visualisation (Thread Intelligence page; SVG)

A clean vertical or horizontal message graph: nodes at chronological positions (ordinal spacing; annotate large gaps like "68 days later"), directed reply arcs (child → parent), timestamps, sender role chips, badges for decision (gold), action, attachment. Nodes are buttons: click scrolls to and highlights the message in the timeline; the selected node and timeline message stay in sync.

